== 2. the CA certificate in the clear, then the API over TLS from a client that trusts it
HTTP/1.1 200 OK
content-type: application/x-x509-ca-cert
content-disposition: attachment; filename="rikuri-node-ca.crt"
-----BEGIN CERTIFICATE-----
-----END CERTIFICATE-----
--- GET /api/setup:
HTTP/1.1 200 OK
cache-control: no-store
{"claimed":false,"claim":"armed","ca_fingerprint_sha256":"1E:A6:45:86:DC:87:75:F0:77:5F:CE:DC:3F:2B:C3:2E:DF:0F:34:57:5E:BC:08:EF:7C:74:20:5D:6D:48:C3:28","names":["localhost","rikuri01.local"]}
== 3. a client without the CA cannot complete the handshake
curl: (60) SSL certificate OpenSSL verify result: unable to get local issuer certificate (20)
curl exit=60 (60 is a certificate problem)
== 4. the plain listener answers an API path with a redirect, never the API
HTTP/1.1 308 Permanent Redirect
location: https://127.0.0.1:18443/api/claim
== 5. the console re-arms the claim (a new code, masked); the old one is dead
rearm exit=0
claim code: [CODE-2]
enter it on the setup page within 24 hours; twenty wrong tries retire it
--- the first code, refused (401):
401
== 6. the claim with the console's code: the owner is made and signed in
HTTP/1.1 201 Created
set-cookie: rikuri_session=[SESSION]; Path=/; Max-Age=2592000; HttpOnly; Secure; SameSite=Strict
--- GET /api/setup after the claim:
{"claimed":true,"claim":"done","ca_fingerprint_sha256":"1E:A6:45:86:DC:87:75:F0:77:5F:CE:DC:3F:2B:C3:2E:DF:0F:34:57:5E:BC:08:EF:7C:74:20:5D:6D:48:C3:28","names":["localhost","rikuri01.local"]}
--- GET /api/session with the cookie (csrf masked):
{"csrf_token":"[CSRF]","issued_unix":1792467832,"expires_unix":1795059832}
--- DELETE /api/session without the CSRF token (403):
403
--- a second claim (409):
409
